{"name":"Basilica Blue","hex":"#4a9fa7","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"basilica","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A Christian church building having a nave with a semicircular apse, side aisles, a narthex and a clerestory.","A Roman Catholic church or cathedral with basilican status, an honorific status granted by the pope to recognize its historical, architectural, or sacramental importance."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/basilica"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Basilica","blocks":[{"text":"In Ancient Roman architecture, a basilica was a large public building with multiple functions that was typically built alongside the town's forum. The basilica was in the Latin West equivalent to a stoa in the Greek East. The building gave its name to the basilica architectural form."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/BasilicaSemproniaReconstruction.jpg/330px-BasilicaSemproniaReconstruction.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basilica","wikidata":"Q163687"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Basilica_Ulpia_J_Guadet_1867.jpg/500px-Basilica_Ulpia_J_Guadet_1867.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Basilica_Ulpia_J_Guadet_1867.jpg/500px-Basilica_Ulpia_J_Guadet_1867.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"19th century reconstruction of the 2nd century AD Basilica Ulpia, part of the Trajan's Forum, Rome","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABasilica_Ulpia_J_Guadet_1867.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/%D4%B5%D6%80%D5%A5%D6%80%D5%B8%D6%82%D5%B5%D6%84%D5%AB_%D5%8F%D5%A1%D5%B3%D5%A1%D6%80_05.jpg/500px-%D4%B5%D6%80%D5%A5%D6%80%D5%B8%D6%82%D5%B5%D6%84%D5%AB_%D5%8F%D5%A1%D5%B3%D5%A1%D6%80_05.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/%D4%B5%D6%80%D5%A5%D6%80%D5%B8%D6%82%D5%B5%D6%84%D5%AB_%D5%8F%D5%A1%D5%B3%D5%A1%D6%80_05.jpg/1280px-%D4%B5%D6%80%D5%A5%D6%80%D5%B8%D6%82%D5%B5%D6%84%D5%AB_%D5%8F%D5%A1%D5%B3%D5%A1%D6%80_05.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/%D4%B5%D6%80%D5%A5%D6%80%D5%B8%D6%82%D5%B5%D6%84%D5%AB_%D5%8F%D5%A1%D5%B3%D5%A1%D6%80_05.jpg/500px-%D4%B5%D6%80%D5%A5%D6%80%D5%B8%D6%82%D5%B5%D6%84%D5%AB_%D5%8F%D5%A1%D5%